1
00:00:00,920 --> 00:00:03,115
Your diagnostic service moves.

2
00:00:03,858 --> 00:00:06,440
The address saved in your client does not.

3
00:00:07,195 --> 00:00:10,327
Suddenly, a routine health check cannot connect.

4
00:00:11,058 --> 00:00:14,225
What if the client could discover the current endpoint,

5
00:00:14,245 --> 00:00:16,265
and verify it before use?

6
00:00:18,570 --> 00:00:20,202
This is DiagDiscover.

7
00:00:20,945 --> 00:00:25,752
A working prototype for DNS based
discovery of diagnostic services.

8
00:00:26,495 --> 00:00:28,715
It brings the published endpoint,

9
00:00:28,820 --> 00:00:33,112
the security checks, and the
diagnostic response into one visible

10
00:00:33,132 --> 00:00:33,878
workflow.

11
00:00:36,237 --> 00:00:39,694
Instead of storing every connection detail in the client,

12
00:00:39,787 --> 00:00:44,042
a publisher advertises service metadata in a signed DNS test

13
00:00:44,062 --> 00:00:44,607
zone.

14
00:00:45,349 --> 00:00:47,444
The client reads the service name,

15
00:00:47,487 --> 00:00:50,569
protocol, and port when it needs to connect.

16
00:00:51,324 --> 00:00:55,494
Our prototype uses a documented DNS-AID-style subset.

17
00:00:57,837 --> 00:00:59,817
Inside the local laboratory,

18
00:00:59,837 --> 00:01:01,967
a publisher, DNS server,

19
00:01:01,987 --> 00:01:06,232
discovery client, and three
diagnostic services work together.

20
00:01:06,987 --> 00:01:10,932
Each experiment creates fresh temporary service endpoints.

21
00:01:11,674 --> 00:01:15,267
The client learns their actual ports from the signed records,

22
00:01:15,287 --> 00:01:17,719
while the dashboard shows the evidence.

23
00:01:20,070 --> 00:01:21,740
Start with the catalogue.

24
00:01:22,482 --> 00:01:24,265
It lists the published health,

25
00:01:24,308 --> 00:01:26,890
latency, and traceroute services.

26
00:01:27,633 --> 00:01:31,965
Each entry includes its advertised endpoint and capability.

27
00:01:32,708 --> 00:01:36,265
This browsing step verifies signed metadata only.

28
00:01:37,020 --> 00:01:40,688
It does not start those services or collect a diagnostic

29
00:01:40,708 --> 00:01:41,540
measurement.

30
00:01:43,903 --> 00:01:47,223
Now choose system health and run normal discovery.

31
00:01:48,016 --> 00:01:52,111
The client checks the service record's DNSSEC signature,

32
00:01:52,191 --> 00:01:56,698
validates its metadata, and
verifies the signed address record.

33
00:01:57,441 --> 00:02:01,708
It then checks the HTTPS certificate
and hostname before sending

34
00:02:01,728 --> 00:02:03,686
the service's access token.

35
00:02:04,441 --> 00:02:08,523
Only an accepted request reaches the diagnostic operation.

36
00:02:10,870 --> 00:02:14,265
The result is a fresh snapshot of CPU activity,

37
00:02:14,295 --> 00:02:16,977
memory usage, and system uptime.

38
00:02:17,720 --> 00:02:20,912
The screen records when the measurement was taken and where

39
00:02:20,932 --> 00:02:21,902
it came from.

40
00:02:22,645 --> 00:02:24,627
In this Docker demonstration,

41
00:02:24,657 --> 00:02:29,000
the values describe the environment
visible to the diagnostic service,

42
00:02:29,020 --> 00:02:31,790
not necessarily Windows Task Manager.

43
00:02:34,137 --> 00:02:37,807
Now change a DNS record after it has been signed.

44
00:02:38,574 --> 00:02:41,194
The first verification stage fails.

45
00:02:41,937 --> 00:02:43,857
Later stages do not run,

46
00:02:43,912 --> 00:02:46,869
and the client sends no diagnostic request.

47
00:02:47,612 --> 00:02:49,692
This is an expected rejection,

48
00:02:49,712 --> 00:02:52,219
making the security boundary visible.

49
00:02:54,570 --> 00:02:58,052
A valid signature is only one part of the decision.

50
00:02:58,808 --> 00:03:02,638
Here, the publisher signs metadata that does not match the

51
00:03:02,658 --> 00:03:04,478
supported service profile.

52
00:03:05,220 --> 00:03:09,478
DNSSEC passes, but the client rejects the metadata.

53
00:03:10,220 --> 00:03:14,365
Authentic information can still be
unsuitable for this application.

54
00:03:16,737 --> 00:03:19,582
Finally, try a wrong access token.

55
00:03:20,312 --> 00:03:23,307
The DNS and HTTPS checks succeed,

56
00:03:23,362 --> 00:03:25,619
but the service refuses access.

57
00:03:26,349 --> 00:03:29,194
Discovery tells the client where to connect.

58
00:03:29,937 --> 00:03:32,894
It does not grant permission to use the service.

59
00:03:33,637 --> 00:03:36,732
That separation is central to our problem statement.

60
00:03:39,070 --> 00:03:41,902
The DNS queries, digital signatures,

61
00:03:41,932 --> 00:03:45,565
HTTPS connections, and token checks are real.

62
00:03:46,308 --> 00:03:48,802
Live health measurements are available.

63
00:03:49,545 --> 00:03:54,002
Latency and traceroute currently
return labelled sample responses.

64
00:03:54,758 --> 00:03:58,302
This is a controlled local prototype with provisioned trust,

65
00:03:58,370 --> 00:04:00,878
not an Internet-wide discovery service.

66
00:04:03,203 --> 00:04:06,673
Built by Chris Itty Biju and Alan Jayan Joseph,

67
00:04:06,766 --> 00:04:09,248
with faculty mentor Revathi BS,

68
00:04:09,353 --> 00:04:12,986
at Karunya University of Technology and Sciences.

69
00:04:13,716 --> 00:04:16,073
DiagDiscover, for AIORI three,

70
00:04:16,103 --> 00:04:18,461
problem statement zero three three.
